<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">h.liu@northeastern.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The pursuit of efficient Large Language Models (LLMs) has led to increasingly complex techniques like extreme quantization and dynamic routing. While individual benefits of these methods are well-documented, their compositional effects remain poorly understood. This paper introduces BitSkip, a hybrid architectural framework for systematically exploring these interactions. Counter-intuitively, our findings reveal that a simple 8-bit quantized model without Hadamard transform (BitSkip-V1) not only outperforms its more complex 4-bit and Hadamard-enhanced counterparts but also competes the full-precision baseline in quality (perplexity of 1.13 vs 1.19). The introduction of Hadamard transforms, even at 8-bit precision, catastrophically degraded performance by over 37,000%, tracing fundamental training instability. Our BitSkip-V1 recipe demonstrates superior early-exit characteristics, with layer 18 providing optimal 32.5% speed gain for minimal 4% quality loss.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id4" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Submitted to Journal of Machine Learning Research
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<table id="p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Keywords</em> Large Language Models  <math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Quantization  <math id="p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Early Exit  <math id="p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Dynamic Neural Networks  <math id="p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Model Efficiency</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The inflation of Large Language Models has reached a point where physical hardware constraints like memory bandwidth, computational throughput, and power consumption increasingly dictate architectural possibilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. This reality has led researchers to shift focus into efficiency techniques, with quantization and dynamic neural networks emerging as two prominent approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Quantization methods have evolved from post-training compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> to extreme co-designed architectures like BitNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> that train models from scratch with 1-bit weights. Simultaneously, dynamic networks like LayerSkip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> have demonstrated that adaptive computation based on input complexity can provide substantial speedups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, a critical gap persists: these techniques have been developed and evaluated in isolation. The fundamental question of how quantization and dynamic routing interact compositionally remains unexplored. Current literature lacks empirical models to predict whether combining efficiency techniques yields multiplicative benefits or destructive interference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces BitSkip, a unified framework for investigating these interactions through systematic ablation across three architectural variants. Our key findings reveal:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(1) Our BitSkip-V1 (8-bit, no Hadamard) achieves superior quality (PPL 1.13 vs 1.19) compared to full-precision baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">(2) Hadamard transforms can be destructive, degrading performance by 37,757% even at 8-bit precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">(3) Early exit viability depends fundamentally on base architecture stability. Our work provides the first comprehensive empirical analysis of quantization-early exit composition, establishing clear design principles for next-generation efficient LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Quantization Techniques</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Quantization reduces the numerical precision of model parameters to improve efficiency. Post-training quantization methods like GPTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and AWQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> enable rapid compression but face accuracy degradation from activation outliers. On the other hand, Quantization-aware training (QAT) as surveyed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nagel et al. [12]</a></cite>, incorporates precision constraints during training, often employing straight-through estimators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> for gradient approximation. This approach consistently outperforms PTQ methods, particularly at aggressive precision levels, though it requires additional computational resources for fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This field has recently witnessed breakthroughs by BitNet family <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> represents a breakthrough in extreme quantization, demonstrating that transformer architectures can be trained from scratch with 1-bit weights while maintaining competitive performance. Bitnet-v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> further introduced Hadamard transformations to enable stable 4-bit activations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Dynamic Neural Networks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dynamic networks adjust computational requirements based on input complexity, recognizing that not all inputs require equal processing. The theoretical foundation was established by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> with methods for gradient propagation through stochastic neurons.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">LayerSkip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> implements this through layer dropout during training with random layer dropout, teaching it to be robust when certain layers are skipped. This creates a network that can function effectively even when using only a subset of its layers. and self-speculative decoding, this technique uses tokens generated by early exits as “draft” predictions. These drafts are then verified in parallel by the full model, allowing for both speed and accuracy. This LayerSkip laid the groundwork for networks that could make discrete decisions about which computational paths to follow. Building on these ideas, early research explored adaptive depth networks and early exiting mechanisms. Recent work like DEL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> has further refined dynamic exit layer selection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>The Composition Gap</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite progress in both domains, the compositional effects of combining quantization with dynamic routing remain unexplored. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> formalized the challenge of “algorithmic speedup” composition but noted the lack of empirical models for predicting interaction effects. Our work directly addresses this gap through systematic experimentation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The BitSkip Framework</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">BitSkip provides a unified framework for investigating quantization-early exit interactions through three architectural variants:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Core Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All BitSkip variants integrate BitLinear layers with LayerSkip’s routing mechanism. The forward pass for layer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{l}=\text{LayerSkip}(\text{BitLinear}(h_{l-1},W_{l}),p_{d}(l))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LayerSkip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>BitLinear</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{l}=\text{LayerSkip}(\text{BitLinear}(h_{l-1},W_{l}),p_{d}(l))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BitLinear}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>BitLinear</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BitLinear}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> implements a precision-constrained linear transformation utilizing ternarized weight matrices <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{l}\in\{-1,0,+1\}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{l}\in\{-1,0,+1\}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. This ternary quantization follows a deterministic rounding scheme during forward propagation while maintaining high-precision gradients during backward passes through straight-through estimators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> for stable training. The ternary constraint in Equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Core Architecture ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> fundamentally alters the computational characteristics, replacing floating-point multiply-accumulate operations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{LayerSkip}(\cdot,p_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>LayerSkip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{LayerSkip}(\cdot,p_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> operator in Equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Core Architecture ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> implements stochastic depth during training and conditional execution during inference. This dynamic routing mechanism executes the internal transformation with probability <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-p_{d}(l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-p_{d}(l)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(l)</annotation></semantics></math> follows a quadratic scheduling function across network depth. During forward passes where the layer is skipped, the operator functions as an identity mapping, passing the input <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged to the subsequent layer. This design enables the network to learn robust representations that remain effective even when arbitrary subsets of layers are bypassed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The quadratic dropout schedule represents a key innovation over prior exponential schedules, defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(l)=c\cdot(l/L)^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(l)=c\cdot(l/L)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a scaling constant calibrated to achieve target early exit rates, <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is the current layer index, and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the total layer count. This quadratic formulation in Equation <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1 Core Architecture ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides smoother gradient flow across layers and improved training stability compared to more aggressive exponential schedules.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For architectures employing Hadamard transformation (BitSkip-V2 and V3), we implement the transform as:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Hadamard}(X)=H_{m}\cdot X" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Hadamard</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Hadamard}(X)=H_{m}\cdot X</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{m}\times 2^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{m}\times 2^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> Hadamard matrix defined recursively as:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{m}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}H_{m-1}&amp;H_{m-1}\\
H_{m-1}&amp;-H_{m-1}\end{bmatrix},\quad H_{0}=(1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{m}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}H_{m-1}&amp;H_{m-1}\\
H_{m-1}&amp;-H_{m-1}\end{bmatrix},\quad H_{0}=(1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. We employ fast Hadamard transform algorithms to perform this multiplication with <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n\log n)</annotation></semantics></math> complexity instead of the naive <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, making the transformation computationally feasible for large-scale language models while preserving its benefits of activation decorrelation and outlier mitigation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Architectural Variants</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BitSkip-V1 (8-bit, No Hadamard):</span> This variant employs 8-bit weight quantization without Hadamard transformation, representing the most straightforward composition of quantization and early exit mechanisms which is similar architecture as that were pioneered and scaled in the BitNet b1.58 work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. The 8-bit precision provides a balanced trade-off between computational efficiency and representation fidelity, maintaining sufficient numerical precision for stable gradient propagation while significantly reducing memory bandwidth requirements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BitSkip-V2 (4-bit + Hadamard):</span> Building upon the BitNet v2 foundation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, this variant incorporates aggressive 4-bit weight quantization coupled with Hadamard transformation for activation preconditioning (Equations <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Core Architecture ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.1 Core Architecture ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). The Hadamard transform serves to decorrelate activation dimensions and reduce the impact of outlier values, theoretically enabling more stable low-precision quantization. This variant represents the most extreme quantization approach, targeting maximum compression while relying on mathematical transformations to maintain representational capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BitSkip-V3 (8-bit + Hadamard):</span> This hybrid variant maintains 8-bit weight quantization while incorporating the Hadamard transformation (Equations <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Core Architecture ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.1 Core Architecture ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), specifically designed to isolate the transform’s independent effects separate from bit-depth reduction. By preserving the same precision as V1 while adding the transform complexity, V3 enables direct assessment of whether activation preconditioning provides benefits even at moderate quantization levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LayerSkip:</span> This variant implements the original LayerSkip architecture (Liu et al., 2024) without quantization, serving as our dynamic routing baseline. It employs full-precision weights with the same quadratic layer dropout schedule and early exit mechanism as other BitSkip variants, enabling direct comparison of how quantization affects dynamic routing performance independent of other architectural changes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Training Methodology</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All models were trained on the TinyStories dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, a corpus of approximately 2.1 million synthetically generated children’s stories. The dataset was tokenized using the GPT-2 BPE tokenizer (vocabulary size: 50,257 tokens) with a maximum sequence length of 512 tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We employed a unified training methodology across all model variants to enable fair comparison, incorporating two key techniques from the LayerSkip framework:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quadratic Layer Dropout:</span> During training, each transformer layer is stochastically dropped with probability <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{l}=p_{\max}\cdot(l/L)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{l}=p_{\max}\cdot(l/L)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is the layer index, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of layers, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\max}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\max}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>. Dropout probabilities are normalized such that <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{l}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{l}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. This progressive dropout schedule ensures that earlier layers receive more training signal, making them suitable for early exit inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Early Exit Loss:</span> All intermediate layers are supervised using a shared language modeling head, following the LayerSkip approach. The total loss is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{total}}=L_{\text{main}}+\lambda\cdot L_{\text{early\_exit}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>main</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>early_exit</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{total}}=L_{\text{main}}+\lambda\cdot L_{\text{early\_exit}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{main}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>main</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{main}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard next-token prediction loss from the final layer, and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{early\_exit}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>early_exit</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{early\_exit}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weighted sum of losses from all intermediate layers:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{early\_exit}}=\sum_{i=1}^{L-1}w_{i}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}(\text{lm\_head}(h_{i}),y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>early_exit</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>lm_head</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{early\_exit}}=\sum_{i=1}^{L-1}w_{i}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}(\text{lm\_head}(h_{i}),y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">with layer-proportional weights <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}=(i+1)/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}=(i+1)/L</annotation></semantics></math> normalized such that <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum w_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum w_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This weighting scheme in Equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.3 Training Methodology ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> gives deeper layers higher influence while ensuring all layers receive meaningful gradients. We set <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.3</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.3 Training Methodology ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> based on ablation studies showing this balances final layer performance with intermediate layer quality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Training Hyperparameters</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameter</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BitSkip-V1, V3 (8-bit)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BitSkip-V2 (4-bit)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Learning rate</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Warmup steps</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1,000</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4,000</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Batch size</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">16</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">16</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Gradient accumulation steps</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Effective batch size</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">64</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">64</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Weight decay</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.1</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.1</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Gradient clipping</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1.0</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Max training steps</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">50,000</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">50,000</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Learning rate schedule</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Linear warmup + decay</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Linear warmup + decay</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Optimizer</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">AdamW</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">AdamW</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.12" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Early exit loss weight (<math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.3</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.3</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.13" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Max dropout probability (<math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.5</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.13.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.5</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantization-Aware Training</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For quantized models (v1, v2, v3), we employed quantization-aware training (QAT) using the Straight-Through Estimator (STE) for gradient flow:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Weight quantization (all quantized variants): Ternary quantization following the BitNet approach:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\text{quant}}=\begin{cases}+\alpha&amp;\text{if }w&gt;0.5\alpha\\
0&amp;\text{if }|w|\leq 0.5\alpha\\
-\alpha&amp;\text{if }w&lt;-0.5\alpha\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>quant</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\text{quant}}=\begin{cases}+\alpha&amp;\text{if }w&gt;0.5\alpha\\
0&amp;\text{if }|w|\leq 0.5\alpha\\
-\alpha&amp;\text{if }w&lt;-0.5\alpha\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\text{mean}(|w|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>mean</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\text{mean}(|w|)</annotation></semantics></math> is the activation scale factor. The ternary weight quantization scheme in Equation <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.3 Training Methodology ‣ 3 The BitSkip Framework ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> ensures that all weight parameters are constrained to three discrete values, significantly reducing memory requirements while maintaining model expressivity through the learned scale factor <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Activation quantization:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">v1, v3 (8-bit): <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{quant}}=\text{round}(\text{clip}(x/s\cdot 127,-128,127))\cdot s/127" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>quant</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>round</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>127</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>128,127</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>127</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{quant}}=\text{round}(\text{clip}(x/s\cdot 127,-128,127))\cdot s/127</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">v2 (4-bit): <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{quant}}=\text{round}(\text{clip}(x/s\cdot 7,-8,7))\cdot s/7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>quant</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>round</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{quant}}=\text{round}(\text{clip}(x/s\cdot 7,-8,7))\cdot s/7</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\max(|x|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\max(|x|)</annotation></semantics></math> is the per-token dynamic scaling factor.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Models and Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate BitSkip variants against a full-precision Llama3 baseline through a two-phase training strategy designed to isolate architectural effects from training methodology.</p>
</div>
