</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Two-Phase Training Strategy</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phase 1 - Standard Training (Intrinsic Analysis):</span> In the first phase, we trained BitSkip-V1, BitSkip-V2, and Llama3 using standard supervised learning without early exit auxiliary losses. This baseline configuration allows us to evaluate the intrinsic capabilities of each architecture independent of dynamic routing effects, providing insights into fundamental quantization stability and transformation behavior. By removing the confounding variable of early exit training, we can directly measure how different quantization levels and transformations affect base model quality and training dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phase 2 - Early Exit Training:</span> Based on Phase 1 results, we identified that BitSkip-V2’s catastrophic failure was due to the Hadamard transformation rather than bit-depth alone. This motivated the design of BitSkip-V3 (8-bit + Hadamard) to isolate the Hadamard effect at moderate precision. In Phase 2, we trained all four variants (V1, V2, V3, and Llama3) with the multi-layer early exit objective described in Section 3.3. Note that BitSkip-V3 was only trained in Phase 2, as it was architected specifically to investigate the compositional effects observed in Phase 1. This enables direct comparison of dynamic routing effectiveness across architectural variants and reveals how early exit training interacts with different quantization strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Intrinsic Architecture Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To decouple the effects of quantization and transformation from the early exit mechanism, we conducted intrinsic evaluations on models trained without early exit losses in Phase 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By removing the confounding variable of early exit training, we can directly measure how different quantization levels (8-bit vs 4-bit) and the Hadamard transformation affect base model quality and training dynamics. This analysis on BitSkip-V1, BitSkip-V2, and Llama3 reveals the fundamental architectural properties that determine whether a model can support effective early exit mechanisms, independent of specialized training techniques.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively assess the performance of our BitSkip variants and establish clear compositional boundaries for efficiency techniques, we employ a multi-faceted evaluation framework that captures both model quality and computational efficiency:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality Metrics:</span> We measure model quality using perplexity (PPL) and cross-entropy loss evaluated on a standardized text corpus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency Metrics:</span> Computational efficiency is quantified through inference speed measured in tokens per second (tok/s).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Early Exit Effectiveness:</span> To evaluate the viability of dynamic routing, we systematically measure the quality-speed tradeoff at intermediate layers 6, 12, 18, and 24.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results and Analysis</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Intrinsic Architecture Performance (Without Early Exit Training)</h3>

<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Intrinsic Model Performance - Standard Training Without Early Exit Loss</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Model</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Bits</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Full Model PPL</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Layer 6 PPL</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Layer 12 PPL</th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Layer 18 PPL</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">BitSkip-V1</th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">8-bit</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1966.49</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">14701.50</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">4330.83</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">2478.66</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">BitSkip-V2</th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4-bit</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">26048.98</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">65109.99</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">49916.51</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">41969.68</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Llama3 FP32</th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">FP32</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">846.34</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">12471.16</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">3059.27</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1246.45</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Intrinsic Architecture Performance (Without Early Exit Training) ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reveals fundamental architectural properties independent of dynamic routing. BitSkip-V1 demonstrates superior early exit characteristics despite higher base perplexity (1966.49 vs 846.34), achieving only 26.0% perplexity increase at layer 18 for 32.4% speed improvement—the best quality-speed ratio (0.80×) among all models. This suggests 8-bit quantization provides sufficient representation stability to support meaningful early exit decisions even without specialized training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">BitSkip-V2’s catastrophic perplexity (26048.98) confirms fundamental training instability from 4-bit quantization with Hadamard transformation, with extremely high values across all layers indicating failure to learn coherent language representations. While Llama3 achieves the best base quality (846.34), it shows massive perplexity increases at shallow layers (1373.5% at layer 6), demonstrating that full-precision models lack natural early exit points without specialized training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Early Exit Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Early Exit Effectiveness with Multi-Layer Training</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Model</th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:42.7pt;">
<span id="S5.T4.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Full Model</span>
<span id="S5.T4.2.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">PPL</span>
</span></th>
