<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2 Early Exit Analysis ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.2 Early Exit Analysis ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> demonstrate BitSkip-V1’s exceptional performance with early exit training, achieving best overall quality (PPL 1.13) with only 4.0% perplexity increase at layer 18 for 32.5% speed gain. The dramatic improvement from intrinsic evaluation (PPL 1.13 vs 1966.49 in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Intrinsic Architecture Performance (Without Early Exit Training) ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) confirms that early exit training stabilizes quantized architectures. Hadamard-enhanced variants (V2, V3) exhibit persistent catastrophic quality issues despite early exit training. Llama3’s 48.3% perplexity increase for 31.2% speed gain demonstrates that early exit training alone cannot match co-designed efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With early exit training, BitSkip-V1 demonstrates exceptional performance, achieving the best overall quality (PPL 1.13) while maintaining viable early exit capabilities. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2 Early Exit Analysis ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the model shows only a 4.0% perplexity increase at layer 18 while gaining 32.5% speed improvement, representing the most favorable quality-speed tradeoff among all architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While Llama3 maintains good absolute quality, its early exit characteristics remain suboptimal, with a 48.3% perplexity increase at layer 18 for only 31.2% speed gain as detailed in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2 Early Exit Analysis ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.2 Early Exit Analysis ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> further confirms this through the quality-speed ratio comparison, demonstrating that simply adding early exit training to full-precision models cannot match the co-designed efficiency of BitSkip-V1.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Activation Variance Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.23766v1/activation_variance.svg" id="S5.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/201;" width="405" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Activation variance (standard deviation) across layer depth for all model variants. Lower and more stable variance indicates better-controlled activation distributions, which correlates with training stability.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the mechanisms behind the observed performance differences, we analyzed activation variance patterns across layer depth (Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5.3 Activation Variance Analysis ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). This analysis reveals that the Hadamard transformation indeed achieves its intended effect of stabilizing activation distributions, but this stabilization alone is insufficient to ensure model quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hadamard Stabilizes Activations but Not Learning.</span> BitSkip-V3 (8-bit + Hadamard) demonstrates the most stable activation variance profile, maintaining values around 1.3-1.5 across all layers after initial growth. Similarly, BitSkip-V2 (4-bit + Hadamard) shows controlled variance that plateaus around layer 5 and gradually decreases in deeper layers. In contrast, BitSkip-V1 exhibits monotonically increasing variance reaching 2.2 at layer 24. Despite this theoretical advantage in activation control, both Hadamard-enhanced variants suffer catastrophic quality degradation (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Intrinsic Architecture Performance (Without Early Exit Training) ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ BitSkip: An Empirical Analysis of Quantization and Early Exit Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), revealing a critical disconnect between activation statistics and actual learning capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Variance-Quality Paradox.</span> The paradox becomes evident when comparing BitSkip-V1 and V3: V3 achieves superior activation stability (1.3 vs 2.2) yet delivers orders of magnitude worse perplexity. This suggests that while the Hadamard transformation successfully decorrelates activations and reduces outliers as designed, it simultaneously disrupts gradient flow or representational capacity in ways that fundamentally impair learning. The divergent behavior of Llama3, with its exponentially growing variance (reaching 5.7), further demonstrates that high activation variance is not inherently problematic when coupled with sufficient numerical precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implications for Quantization Design.</span> These findings challenge the conventional wisdom that activation stabilization is universally beneficial for quantized models. The success of BitSkip-V1 with its higher, unstable variance suggests that 8-bit precision provides sufficient headroom to accommodate natural activation growth, while aggressive stabilization through Hadamard transforms may overconstrain the representational dynamics necessary for effective learning. This points to a critical design principle: optimization objectives should target task performance rather than intermediate statistical properties.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work introduces BitSkip, a systematic framework for investigating quantization-early exit composition in large language models. Our most significant finding demonstrates that simpler architectural choices—specifically 8-bit quantization without Hadamard transformation—can outperform both complex variants and full-precision baselines. BitSkip-V1 achieves superior quality (PPL 1.13 vs 1.19) while maintaining exceptional early exit characteristics, with only 4% quality degradation for 32.5% speed gains at layer 18.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The catastrophic failure of Hadamard-enhanced variants (V2, V3), despite achieving superior activation stabilization, reveals a critical disconnect between intermediate statistical properties and actual learning capacity. This variance-quality paradox challenges conventional assumptions in quantization design, suggesting that optimization should target task performance rather than presumed beneficial mathematical properties. Our activation variance analysis demonstrates that while Hadamard transforms successfully decorrelate activations as intended, they simultaneously disrupt representational dynamics necessary for effective learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These findings establish clear design principles for next-generation efficient LLMs: (1) technique composition requires empirical validation rather than theoretical extrapolation, (2) architectural simplicity can transcend complexity when properly co-designed with efficiency mechanisms, and (3) early exit viability depends fundamentally on base architecture stability rather than specialized training alone.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Limitations and Future Work</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study has several limitations that present opportunities for future research. First, our experiments were conducted exclusively on the TinyStories dataset, a relatively small corpus of synthetic children’s stories. While this controlled setting enabled systematic ablation studies, it may not fully capture the training dynamics and compositional effects that emerge with larger, more diverse datasets typical of production LLM training. The generalization of our findings to multi-billion token corpora and diverse domains remains an open question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Second, our architectural exploration focused on three specific BitSkip variants. The design space of quantization-dynamic routing compositions is vast, including alternative quantization schemes (e.g., mixed-precision, group quantization), different routing mechanisms (e.g., confidence-based exits, learned routing), and other activation transformations beyond Hadamard.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Future work should investigate: (1) scaling these findings to larger datasets and model sizes to validate whether the observed patterns hold at scale, (2) exploring the theoretical foundations of why Hadamard transforms interfere with learning despite stabilizing activations, (3) developing predictive models for technique composition that can guide architectural design without exhaustive empirical search, and (4) extending the analysis to other efficiency techniques such as pruning, distillation, and mixture-of-experts architectures.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thank the reviewers for constructive feedback that improved this work’s clarity and rigor. Computational resources were provided by Northeastern University’s Discovery Cluster.</p>
</div>
</section>
